    if (Boolean.TRUE.equals(ownerFieldsDto.getSharesHousehold())) {
        clinicService.shareHousehold(owner);
    }
    if (clinicService.countOwnersByCity(owner.getCity()) >= MAX_OWNERS_PER_CITY) {
        return new ResponseEntity<>(HttpStatus.CONFLICT);
    }
    // A single derived identity key now captures the telephone, email and household
    // duplicate checks: only an exact whole-key match with an existing owner is a
    // duplicate, so two household members with different telephones both remain valid.
    if (clinicService.existsOwnerByIdentityKey(owner.getIdentityKey())) {
        return new ResponseEntity<>(HttpStatus.CONFLICT);
    }
    this.clinicService.saveOwner(owner);
    this.ownerAuditLogger.logCreated(owner);
    OwnerDto ownerDto = ownerMapper.toOwnerDto(owner);
    headers.setLocation(UriComponentsBuilder.newInstance()
        .path("/api/owners/{id}").buildAndExpand(owner.getId()).toUri());
    return new ResponseEntity<>(ownerDto, headers, HttpStatus.CREATED);
}
```
The route (`POST /api/owners`) and the `OwnerFieldsDto` request / `OwnerDto` response types come from `OwnersApi` (generated from the spec). The class base path is `/api` (`@RequestMapping("/api")`). This is the single relevant create endpoint. `OwnerRestControllerV2` (`/home/daniel/sandbox/.../rest/controller/v2/OwnerRestControllerV2.java`) only adds a paged `listOwnersPage` GET — no create.

This is where hard-duplicate handling lives today (the `existsOwnerByIdentityKey` → 409 block), and the natural home for adding soft-duplicate flagging, since it already has the `owner` and the `clinicService`.

---

## 3. Service layer
Interface: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/service/ClinicService.java`
Impl: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/service/ClinicServiceImpl.java`
Audit side-effect: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/service/OwnerAuditLogger.java`

Owner-creation-relevant `ClinicService` methods: `saveOwner(Owner)`, `existsOwnerByIdentityKey(String)`, `countOwnersByCity(String)`, `countOwnersByRegistrationDate(LocalDate)`, `shareHousehold(Owner)`, `findOwnerById(int)`, `findOwnerByLastName(String)`, `findAllOwners()`.

Key `ClinicServiceImpl` methods:

`saveOwner` (lines 245-261) — on new owners it stamps `customerCode` (via `customerCodeGenerator`), `namesakeCount` (via `countNamesakes`), `bulkSignupWarning`, and `householdSize`, then delegates to `ownerRepository.save`:
```java
@Override
@Transactional
public void saveOwner(Owner owner) throws DataAccessException {
    if (owner.isNew()) {
        if (owner.getCustomerCode() == null) {
            owner.setCustomerCode(customerCodeGenerator.generate(
                RegionResolver.regionFor(owner.getPostcode(), owner.getCity()),
                owner.getTelephone(), owner.getLastName()));
        }
        owner.setNamesakeCount(countNamesakes(owner.getFirstName(), owner.getLastName()));
        owner.setBulkSignupWarning(
            ownerRepository.countByRegistrationDate(owner.getRegistrationDate()) > BULK_SIGNUP_WARNING_THRESHOLD);
        owner.setHouseholdSize(householdSizeIncluding(owner));
    }
    ownerRepository.save(owner);
}
```

`existsOwnerByIdentityKey` (lines 292-297) — the current (hard) duplicate check, an in-memory scan over all owners:
```java
@Override
@Transactional(readOnly = true)
public boolean existsOwnerByIdentityKey(String identityKey) throws DataAccessException {
    return ownerRepository.findAll().stream()
        .anyMatch(existing -> identityKey.equals(existing.getIdentityKey()));
}
```

`shareHousehold` (lines 311-324) and its private helper `householdMembers` (330-336) use `findByLastNameIgnoreCase` + `householdIdGenerator` to group owners by last name + address. `countNamesakes` (280-284) also filters `findByLastNameIgnoreCase`. These are the closest existing analogues to a "same lastName + postcode" soft-match query.

`OwnerAuditLogger.logCreated(Owner)` logs to the `AUDIT` logger after a successful create.

---

## 4. Owner repository
Interface: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/repository/OwnerRepository.java`
Spring Data impl: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/repository/springdatajpa/SpringDataOwnerRepository.java`
Other profile impls: `.../repository/jpa/JpaOwnerRepositoryImpl.java`, `.../repository/jdbc/JdbcOwnerRepositoryImpl.java`.

Existing query methods on the interface: `findByLastName(String)`, `findByLastName(String, Pageable)`, `findById(int)`, `findByLastNameIgnoreCase(String)`, `save(Owner)`, `count()`, `countByCity(String)`, `countByRegistrationDate(LocalDate)`, `countByHouseholdId(String)`, `findAll()`, `findAll(Pageable)`, `delete(Owner)`.

There is **no** postcode-based finder and no `findByLastNameAndPostcode`-style query — a soft-duplicate lookup would need a new method here (and in all three profile impls). The Spring Data impl provides `@Query` overrides for most methods; `findByLastNameIgnoreCase` is derived automatically from its name (not explicitly annotated). Note the JDBC/JPA/SpringData split is profile-driven (`@Profile("spring-data-jpa")` etc.), so any new repository method must be added to the `OwnerRepository` interface and each active profile implementation.

---

## 5. Existing duplicate-detection logic
- Controller hard check: `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner` lines 180-185 (`existsOwnerByIdentityKey` → 409 CONFLICT), comment at 180-182.
- Identity-key definition: `Owner.getIdentityKey()` lines 252-265 (`telephone|email|householdId`).
- Service: `ClinicServiceImpl.existsOwnerByIdentityKey` lines 292-297; `ClinicService.existsOwnerByIdentityKey` javadoc lines 64-72.
- Household grouping (a soft-ish grouping already): `shareHousehold` / `householdMembers` and the `sharesHousehold` write-only flag which *skips* the household duplicate check.
- No `possibleDuplicate` / `possibleDuplicateOf` implementation exists in `src/main` or `openapi.yml` (confirmed by grep). The only occurrences are in the test `AcceptanceTest.java` and its comments.

---

## 6. DTOs / response objects for create
The create endpoint consumes `OwnerFieldsDto` and returns `OwnerDto` — both generated from `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/openapi.yml` into package `org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.dto`. Mapping is done by MapStruct:

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/mapper/OwnerMapper.java`
```java
@Mapper(uses = PetMapper.class)
public interface OwnerMapper {
    @Mapping(target = "displayName", expression = "java(owner.getLastName() + \", \" + owner.getFirstName())")
    OwnerDto toOwnerDto(Owner owner);
    Owner toOwner(OwnerDto ownerDto);
    @Mapping(target = "id", ignore = true)
    @Mapping(target = "pets", ignore = true)
    @Mapping(target = "customerCode", ignore = true)
    Owner toOwner(OwnerFieldsDto ownerDto);
    List<OwnerDto> toOwnerDtoCollection(Collection<Owner> ownerCollection);
    Collection<Owner> toOwners(Collection<OwnerDto> ownerDtos);
    default OwnerPageDto toOwnerPageDto(@NonNull Page<Owner> ownerPage) { ... }
}
```
MapStruct auto-maps the entity's `@Transient` getters (customerCode, checkDigit, membershipNumber, membershipLevel, locality, householdId, namesakeCount, bulkSignupWarning, contactPreference, identityKey, ageBand, initials) onto the `Owner` schema fields, so any new `possibleDuplicate`/`possibleDuplicateOf` fields would need to be added to the spec AND either the entity (as a transient/persistent field) or an explicit `@Mapping`.

Relevant `openapi.yml` schemas (`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/openapi.yml`):
- `OwnerFields` (lines 1991-2089): input fields firstName, lastName, address, city, telephone, email, postcode, registrationDate, birthDate, `sharesHousehold` (write-only). Required: firstName, lastName, address, city, telephone.
- `Owner` (lines 2090-2232): `allOf` OwnerFields + read-only id, pets, displayName, initials, customerCode, checkDigit, householdId, namesakeCount, membershipNumber, membershipLevel, locality, bulkSignupWarning, contactPreference, identityKey, ageBand. **No possibleDuplicate/possibleDuplicateOf**.
- `OwnerPage` (lines 2233-2277).

---

## 7. Tests for owner creation
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceTest.java`
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceBase.java`
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AuditLogCapture.java`

`AcceptanceTest.coreFlagsPossibleDuplicate` is the directly relevant test (currently failing against production code, since the fields don't exist):
```java
@Test
void coreFlagsPossibleDuplicate() throws Exception {
    String lastName = uniqueLastName();
    ObjectNode a = withPostcode(ownerNode());
    a.put("lastName", lastName);
    int ida = createOwnerOk(a);
    ObjectNode b = withPostcode(ownerNode()); // same postcode + lastName, unique telephone/address
    b.put("lastName", lastName);
    int idb = createOwnerOk(b);
    getOwner(idb).andExpect(jsonPath("$.possibleDuplicate").value(true))
            .andExpect(jsonPath("$.possibleDuplicateOf").value(ida));
}
```
How it asserts: it POSTs two owners sharing `lastName` + postcode `2000` but with unique telephone/address (so *not* a hard identity-key duplicate — both are created, 2xx), then does `GET /api/owners/{idb}` and asserts via JSONPath that the second owner's response body has `possibleDuplicate == true` and `possibleDuplicateOf == ida` (the first owner's id).

`AcceptanceBase` (SpringBootTest + MockMvc, `@Transactional` rollback, `@WithMockUser` admin) drives everything through `POST /api/owners` and reads back via `GET /api/owners/{id}`. Helpers of note: `ownerNode()` (fully-unique valid owner), `withPostcode()` (adds `"postcode":"2000"`), `createOwnerOk()` (asserts 2xx, extracts id from body `id` or `Location` header), `getOwner()`/`fetchOwner()`, and `deleteOwner()` (soft-delete — its javadoc says deleted records are retained and ignored by the create endpoint's duplicate/identity checks). Note: soft-match is keyed on **lastName + postcode with a different telephone**, distinct from the existing hard identity key (telephone|email|householdId).

`AuditLogCapture.java` is a test helper for asserting audit-log output (from `OwnerAuditLogger`).

---

## 8. Build / test
- **Maven**, wrapper present: `/home/daniel/sandbox/mvnw` (+ `.cmd`) and `/home/daniel/sandbox/.mvn/`. Build file: `/home/daniel/sandbox/pom.xml` (artifactId `spring-petclinic-rest`, Spring Boot starter parent). No Gradle files.
- Build: `./mvnw clean package`; tests: `./mvnw test`. JaCoCo coverage is bound (prepare-agent/report/check executions in pom).
- DTO/API generation: `openapi-generator-maven-plugin` `generate` goal, `inputSpec = src/main/resources/openapi.yml`, `modelNameSuffix = Dto`, `apiPackage = ...rest.api`, `modelPackage = ...rest.dto`, added via `build-helper` `add-source`. So editing `openapi.yml` is required to add response fields; generated sources appear under `target/generated-sources` (not present now — repo is unbuilt).
- Persistence is profile-selected (`spring-data-jpa` / `jpa` / `jdbc`); H2/HSQLDB/MySQL/Postgres drivers are on the classpath.

The natural place to implement soft-duplicate detection: a new repository finder (same lastName + postcode) added to `OwnerRepository` and its impls, a service method on `ClinicService`/`ClinicServiceImpl`, new `possibleDuplicate`/`possibleDuplicateOf` fields in `openapi.yml`'s `Owner` schema and on the `Owner` entity (or via `OwnerMapper`), and wiring in `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner` right around the existing hard-duplicate block (lines 174-186).

